}&item[0][description]=HTTP has multiple request "verbs", such as `GET`, `PUT`, `POST`, `DELETE`,
`PATCH`, `HEAD`, etc.

An HTTP Method (verb) defines how a request should be interpreted by a server.
The endpoints in this section demonstrate various HTTP Verbs. Postman supports
all the HTTP Verbs, including some rarely used ones, such as `PROPFIND`, `UNLINK`,
etc.

For details about HTTP Verbs, refer to [RFC 2616](http://www.w3.org/Protocols/rfc2616/rfc2616-sec9.html#sec9)
&item[1][name]=Headers&item[1][item][0][name]=Request Headers&item[1][item][0][event][0][listen]=test&item[1][item][0][event][0][script][type]=text/javascript&item[1][item][0][event][0][script][exec][]=pm.test("response is ok", function () {&item[1][item][0][event][0][script][exec][]=    pm.response.to.have.status(200);&item[1][item][0][event][0][script][exec][]=});&item[1][item][0][event][0][script][exec][]=&item[1][item][0][event][0][script][exec][]=// we check to ensire that the headers we sent as request is returned as part of&item[1][item][0][event][0][script][exec][]=// the response body. if you change the request headers, make sure to add them&item[1][item][0][event][0][script][exec][]=// to the tests here&item[1][item][0][event][0][script][exec][]=pm.test("sample request header should be returned in response body", function () {&item[1][item][0][event][0][script][exec][]=    pm.response.to.have.jsonBody('headers.my-sample-header', 'Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet');&item[1][item][0][event][0][script][exec][]=});&item[1][item][0][request][method]=GET&item[1][item][0][request][header][0][key]=my-sample-header&item[1][item][0][request][header][0][value]=Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet&item[1][item][0][request][body][mode]=formdata&item[1][item][0][request][url][raw]=https://postman-echo.com/headers&item[1][item][0][request][url][protocol]=https&item[1][item][0][request][url][host][]=postman-echo&item[1][item][0][request][url][host][]=com&item[1][item][0][request][url][path][]=headers&item[1][item][0][request][description]=A `GET` request to this endpoint returns the list of all request headers as part of the response JSON.
