s.org>&item[1][item][1][response][0][header][11][name]=X-Powered-By&item[1][item][1][response][0][header][11][description]=&item[1][item][1][response][0][header][12][key]=test&item[1][item][1][response][0][header][12][value]=response_headers&item[1][item][1][response][0][header][12][name]=test&item[1][item][1][response][0][header][12][description]=&item[1][item][1][response][0][cookie][0][expires]=Invalid Date&item[1][item][1][response][0][cookie][0][hostOnly]=true&item[1][item][1][response][0][cookie][0][httpOnly]=true&item[1][item][1][response][0][cookie][0][domain]=echo.getpostman.com&item[1][item][1][response][0][cookie][0][path]=/&item[1][item][1][response][0][cookie][0][secure]=false&item[1][item][1][response][0][cookie][0][session]=true&item[1][item][1][response][0][cookie][0][value]=s%3A9stja5zKmIILxq9Jvtha7Lp9LIz1VIdK.Vp8MHC%2BEUJe4ICZPXn2JAoXaV2bTgtoQd%2B3XJLNr51Y&item[1][item][1][response][0][cookie][0][key]=sails.sid&item[1][item][1][response][0][body]={"Content-Type":"text/html","test":"response_headers"}&item[1][description]=The following set of endpoints allow one to see the headers being sent as part of a request and to get a custom set of headers as part of response.

HTTP header fields provide required information about the request or response, or about the object sent in the message body. Both request headers and response headers can be controlled using these endpoints.&item[2][name]=Authentication Methods&item[2][item][0][name]=Basic Auth Success&item[2][item][0][event][0][listen]=test&item[2][item][0][event][0][script][type]=text/javascript&item[2][item][0][event][0][script][exec][]=pm.test("response is ok", function () {&item[2][item][0][event][0][script][exec][]=    pm.response.to.have.status(200);&item[2][item][0][event][0][script][exec][]=});&item[2][item][0][event][0][script][exec][]=&item[2][item][0][event][0][script][exec][]=pm.test("response body has json saying 'authenticated'", function () {&item[2][item][0][event][0][script][exec][]=    pm.response.to.have.jsonBody('authenticated', true);&item[2][item][0][event][0][script][exec][]=});&item[2][item][0][request][auth][type]=basic&item[2][item][0][request][auth][basic][0][key]=username&item[2][item][0][request][auth][basic][0][value]=postman&item[2][item][0][request][auth][basic][0][type]=string&item[2][item][0][request][auth][basic][1][key]=password&item[2][item][0][request][auth][basic][1][value]=password&item[2][item][0][request][auth][basic][1][type]=string&item[2][item][0][request][auth][basic][2][key]=saveHelperData&item[2][item][0][request][auth][basic][2][type]=any&item[2][item][0][request][auth][basic][3][key]=showPassword&item[2][item][0][request][auth][basic][3][value]=false&item[2][item][0][request][auth][basic][3][type]=boolean&item[2][item][0][request][method]=GET&item[2][item][0][request][body][mode]=formdata&item[2][item][0][request][url][raw]=https://postman-echo.com/basic-auth&item[2][item][0][request][url][protocol]=https&item[2][item][0][request][url][host][]=postman-echo&item[2][item][0][request][url][host][]=com&item[2][item][0][request][url][path][]=basic-auth&item[2][item][0][request][description]=This endpoint simulates a **basic-auth** protected endpoint.
The endpoint accepts a default username and password and returns a status code of `200 ok` only if the same is provided.
Otherwise it will return a status code `401 unauthorized`.

> Username: `postman`
>
> Password: `password`

To use this endpoint, send a request with the header `Authorization: Basic cG9zdG1hbjpwYXNzd29yZA==`.
The cryptic latter half of the header value is a base64 encoded concatenation of the default username and password.
Using Postman, to send this request, you can simply fill in the username and password in the "Authorization" tab and Postman will do the rest for you.

To know more about basic authentication, refer to the [Basic Access Authentication](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basic_access_authentication) wikipedia article.
The article on [authentication helpers](https://www.getpostman.com/docs/helpers#basic-auth?source=echo-collection-app-onboarding) elaborates how to use the same within the Postman app.&item[2][item][0][response][0][name]=200&item[2][item][0][response][0][originalRequest][auth][type]=basic&item[2][item][0][response][0][originalRequest][auth][basic][0][key]=username&item[2][item][0][response][0][originalRequest][auth][basic][0][value]=postman&item[2][item][0][response][0][originalRequest][auth][basic][0][type]=string&item[2][item][0][response][0][originalRequest][auth][basic][1][key]=password&item[2][item][0][response][0][originalRequest][auth][basic][1][value]=password&item[2][item][0][response][0][originalRequest][auth][basic][1][type]=string&item[2][item][0][response][0][originalRequest][auth][basic][2][key]=saveHelperData&item[2][item][0][response][0][originalRequest][auth][basic][2][type]=any&item[2][item][0][response][0][originalRequest][auth][basic][3][key]=showPassword&item[2][item][0][response][0][originalRequest][auth][basic][3][value]=false&item[2][item][0][response][0][originalRequest][auth][basic][3][type]=boolean&item[2][item][0][response][0][originalRequest][method]=GET&item[2][item][0][response][0][originalRequest][body][mode]=formdata&item[2][item][0][response][0][originalRequest][url][raw]=https://postman-echo.com/basic-auth&item[2][item][0][response][0][originalRequest][url][protocol]=https&item[2][item][0][response][0][originalRequest][url][host][]=postman-echo&item[2][item][0][response][0][originalRequest][url][host][]=com&item[2][item][0][response][0][originalRequest][url][path][]=basic-auth&item[2][item][0][response][0][originalRequest][description]=This endpoint simulates a **basic-auth** protected endpoint.
The endpoint accepts a default username and password and returns a status code of `200 ok` only if the same is provided.
Otherwise it will return a status code `401 unauthorized`.

> Username: `postman`
>
> Password: `password`

To use this endpoint, send a request with the header `Authorization: Basic cG9zdG1hbjpwYXNzd29yZA==`.
The cryptic latter half of the header value is a base64 encoded concatenation of the default username and password.
Using Postman, to send this request, you can simply fill in the username and password in the "Authorization" tab and Postman will do the rest for you.

To know more about basic authentication, refer to the [Basic Access Authentication](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basic_access_authentication) wikipedia article.
